<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Match Quadratic Graphs and Their Equations: Mini-Lesson Review</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02075</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Match Quadratic Graphs and Their Equations: Mini-Lesson Review</md:title>
    <md:uuid>0d1ad1cc-34df-4da2-a236-528a9d8823ab</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Match Quadratic Graphs and Their Equations</title>
      <para>The factored form helps find the zeros of a quadratic function.</para>
      <para>The factored form gives the zeros, and the \(x\)-intercepts are located where each factor equals 0.</para>
      <para>
        <emphasis>Example</emphasis>
      </para>
      <para>Examine the graph. What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph?</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UPWARD WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 4 AND \(x\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 4 AND 1.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_02_08_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
      </para>
      <para>The graph crosses the \(x\)-axis at \(x = -4\) and \(x = 1\). These are the \(x\)-intercepts.</para>
      <para>Look at each function to see which matches the graph. Look for the factors that have zeros as \(x = -4\) and \(x = 1\).</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
        <item>\(f(x) = (x + 4)(x + 1)\)</item>
      </list>
      <list>
        <item> This function has zeros at \(x = -4\) and \(x = -1\). The zero \(x = -1\) is not a zero on the graph. </item>
      </list>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
        <item>\(f(x) = (x - 4)(x - 1)\)</item>
      </list>
      <list>
        <item> This function has zeros at \(x = 4\) and \(x = 1\). The zero \(x = 4\) is not a zero on the graph. </item>
      </list>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
        <item>\(f(x) = (x - 4)(x + 1)\)</item>
      </list>
      <list>
        <item> This function has zeros at \(x = 4\) and \(x = -1\). The signs of the zeros of this function are the opposite of the signs of the zeros on the graph. </item>
      </list>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
        <item>\(f(x) = (x + 4)(x - 1)\)</item>
      </list>
      <list>
        <item> This function has zeros at \(x = -4\) and \(x = -1\). This function has zeros that match the zeros on the graph. </item>
      </list>
      <para>The function \(f(x) = (x + 4)(x - 1)\) matches the graph.</para>
      <title>Try It: Match Quadratic Graphs and Their Equations</title>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Examine the graph.</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UPWARD WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 8 AND \(x\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 2 AND 4.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_02_08_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>Which function matches the graph?</para>
            <para>\(f(x) = (x + 2)(x + 4)\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x) = (x - 2)(x - 4)\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x) = (x + 2)(x - 4)\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x) = (x - 2)(x + 4)\)</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Here’s how to match the function to the graph.</para>
            <para>Look at each function to see which matches the graph. Look for the factors that have zeros at \(x = -2\) and \(x = 4\).</para>
            <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
              <item>\(f(x) = (x + 2)(x + 4)\)</item>
            </list>
            <list>
              <item> This function has zeros at \(x = -2\) and \(x = -4\). The zero \(x = -4\) is not a zero on the graph. </item>
            </list>
            <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
              <item>\(f(x) = (x - 2)(x - 4)\)</item>
            </list>
            <list>
              <item> This function has zeros at \(x = 2\) and \(x = 4\). The zero \(x = 2\) is not a zero on the graph. </item>
            </list>
            <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
              <item>\(f(x) = (x + 2)(x - 4)\)</item>
            </list>
            <list>
              <item> This function has zeros at \(x = -2\) and \(x = 4\). This function has zeros that match the zeros on the graph. </item>
            </list>
            <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
              <item>\(f(x) = (x - 2)(x + 4)\)</item>
            </list>
            <list>
              <item> This function has zeros at \(x = 2\) and \(x = -4\). The zeros of the function have the opposite signs of the zeros on the graph. </item>
            </list>
            <para>The function \(f(x) = (x + 2)(x - 4)\) matches the graph.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Check Your Understanding</title>
        <exercise data-content-id="85e6ea4a-1b2d-4462-93b2-3e7ee9cbb4b7" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Examine the graph.</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UPWARD WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 6 AND \(x\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 3 AND 2.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_02_08_03.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>Which function matches the graph?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(f(x) = (x - 3)(x - 2)\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x) = (x - 3)(x + 2)\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x) = (x + 3)(x - 2)\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x) = (x + 3)(x + 2)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Take a moment to think about what you learned in the mini-lesson review. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
